        "On the shore of Long An Lake in Buu Long ward, Tran Bien Temple of Literature was commissioned by Lord Nguyen Phuc Chu in 1715 to honour Confucius and promote learning, making it the earliest such temple in the South. Destroyed during wartime, it was rebuilt on its old foundations and re-inaugurated in 2002. The spacious complex blends traditional royal architecture with lakes and gardens. From the main gate visitors pass the Khue Van pavilion, stele houses, a bridge and the central ceremonial hall, which honours President Ho Chi Minh together with the nation's great cultural figures and distinguished scholars. The stele houses celebrate the tradition of study and the outstanding sons of the Bien Hoa – Dong Nai region. With its lotus pond, shaded walkways and ancient trees, the temple is both a place of historical and cultural significance and a pleasant spot to stroll, take photographs and learn about the scholarly heritage of southern Vietnam.",
