        "In Loc Ninh town, Loc Ninh district (former Binh Phuoc, now part of Dong Nai), the Loc Ninh Reception House is a notable historical relic tied to the 1972–1975 period, when Loc Ninh became the first district in the South to be liberated and was regarded as the “capital” of the Provisional Revolutionary Government of the Republic of South Vietnam. The house was built to receive delegations, international missions and the International Commission overseeing implementation of the Paris Agreement. In particular, the Loc Ninh area is linked to the exchange of prisoners of war and political prisoners between the parties after the 1973 Paris Agreement, an important milestone in history. The reception house has the simple architecture of the war years and is now preserved with artefacts, documents and images illustrating that heroic period. Part of the Loc Ninh revolutionary relic cluster (together with the Loc Ninh airfield and sites related to the fuel pipeline and depots), the Reception House is a meaningful destination for those wishing to learn about the liberated zone and the pages of struggle of the soldiers and people of the south-east in the north of the new Dong Nai province.",
